  "visualDNA": {
    "Overview": "Veiqia (also referred to as weniqia) is a traditional form of tattooing that was exclusive to women in Fiji. The term can refer both to the practice and to the tattoos themselves. Kingsley Roth, a British colonial administrator, described in his 1933 publication that veiqia was marked onto young women's bodies at the time of puberty or sometimes at the onset of menstruation. The practice demonstrated that the women were available for marriage and had physically reached sexual maturity. Typically, young women would receive veiqia in the groin and on the buttocks."
